WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:31.270
<v Ira Glass>Consider for a moment all the art forms that were created on this continent by Americans-- jazz, the blues, musical theater, rock and roll, phonograph recordings themselves, television, motion pictures, video games. I would argue that all of them are eclipsed by the art form at which we truly excel as a nation, the art form which more Americans practice than any other, the art form which actually dominates all the other art forms and our political life as well. And that is the art of selling. That's what we do the most reliably.
<v Ira Glass>试想一下，美国人在这个大陆上创造的所有艺术形式——爵士乐、蓝调、音乐剧、摇滚乐、留声机唱片、电视、电影、电子游戏。我认为，所有这些艺术形式都比不上我们作为一个国家真正擅长的艺术形式，这种艺术形式比其他任何形式都有更多的美国人在实践，它实际上主宰了所有其他艺术形式，也主宰了我们的政治生活。那就是销售的艺术。这是我们做得最可靠的事情。

00:00:31.270 --> 00:01:08.430
<v Ira Glass>From Wall Street to the pollsters in market research firms and focus group operations all across the fruited plain, from the telemarketers who phone us during dinner to the junk mailing operations that fill our mailboxes in the morning, from James Carville selling candidates on our televisions to the gang kids on Chicago's West Side selling weed in Humboldt Park, from the corny ads on every other radio station to the pledge drives that I participate in myself on this very station. From sea to shining sea, sales is our great democratic art form. And I would argue, nothing wrong with that. And in any case, we don't have any choice about it, so we might as well decide to feel good about the whole thing.
<v Ira Glass>从华尔街到市场研究公司和焦点小组运作的民调专家，从全美各地在晚餐时间给我们打电话的电话推销员，到每天早上填满我们信箱的垃圾邮件，从在电视上兜售候选人的詹姆斯·卡维尔，到芝加哥西区在洪堡公园卖大麻的帮派少年，从每个电台播放的俗气广告，到我本人也参与的这一电台的认捐活动。从大海到大海，销售是我们伟大的民主艺术形式。我认为，这没什么不对。而且无论如何，我们对此别无选择，所以我们不妨决定对这一切感觉良好。

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:29.150
<v Ira Glass>To help you do that, today on our radio program, inspiring case studies and some horrifying ones too. The whole gamut. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>为了帮助你们做到这一点，今天我们的广播节目将介绍一些鼓舞人心的案例，也有一些令人毛骨悚然的案例。应有尽有。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台和国际公共广播电台，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:46.190
<v Ira Glass>Today's program, Sales. America's greatest art form. Act One of our show today, How To Talk Your Way Into Half A Million Dollars In Just 45 Minutes. In that act, Sandra Tsing Loh accompanies a Hollywood screenwriter, as he tries to sell a movie idea.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目主题是：销售。美国最伟大的艺术形式。我们今天节目的第一幕，“如何在45分钟内说服他人给你五十万美元”。在这一幕中，桑德拉·青·罗陪伴一位好莱坞编剧尝试推销一个电影构思。

00:01:46.190 --> 00:02:01.780
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Jail Sell. That's S-E-L-L. A story from Danny Hoch in which a true American salesman gets thrown in the slammer for simply being an entrepreneur and has a few words to say about it, believe me.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“监狱推销”。拼写是S-E-L-L。这是丹尼·霍克讲述的一个故事，一位真正的美国推销员因为仅仅是做了一个创业者就被关进了监狱，他对此有一些话要说，相信我。

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:35.770
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Who's The Man? We have this story of how becoming a salesman can change your life, make you more confident, make you able to charm anyone. And why you might decide that that's not such a good thing. Act Four, The Secret To Being Rich And Happy. The life of 76-year-old salesman Jimmy Roy in Braddock, Pennsylvania raises the possibility that being a rich salesman and being a happy salesmen might be mutually exclusive. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“谁才是大佬？”我们有这样一个故事：成为一名推销员如何改变你的生活，让你更自信，让你能够吸引任何人。以及为什么你可能会觉得这并不是什么好事。第四幕，“富有且快乐的秘诀”。76岁的推销员吉米·罗在宾夕法尼亚州布拉多克的生活引发了一种可能性，即成为一名富有的推销员和成为一名快乐的推销员可能是相互排斥的。请继续收听。

00:02:35.770 --> 00:03:01.400
<v Ira Glass>Act One, How To Talk Your Way Into A Half Million Dollars In Just 45 Minutes. Friends, let us consider one of the many crossroads where art and commerce meet and ask the question, is commerce the greater art than art itself? In our first story today, writer Sandra Tsing Loh takes us into a setting that, as an occasional screenwriter, she knows well herself-- the Hollywood pitch meeting, where movie and TV deals are made.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，如何在45分钟内说服他人给你五十万美元。朋友们，让我们思考一下艺术与商业相遇的众多十字路口之一，并问一个问题：商业是否比艺术本身更伟大？在今天的第一篇报道中，作家桑德拉·青·罗带我们进入了一个她作为偶尔从事编剧工作的人非常熟悉的场景——好莱坞推销会议，电影和电视交易就是在这里达成的。

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:19.620
<v Ira Glass>And we thought we would start our show here because it is a sales situation where the stakes are enormously high, where things are decided very quickly, and where people who often like to think of themselves as artists-- I mean, writers, and producers, and people like that-- are basically forced to be salesmen. Here's Sandra.
<v Ira Glass>我们想从这里开始我们的节目，因为这是一个赌注极高的销售情境，事情决定得非常快，而且那些经常自认为是艺术家的人——我是说，作家、制片人等等——基本上被迫成为推销员。这是桑德拉。

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:45.540
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>I'm with writer Scott Fifer two days before a big movie pitch meeting. We're in his small office nested deep in an office building on Wilshire, where Scott works on the writing staff of Beverly Hills, 90210, a waning Spelling TV show about increasingly long-in-the-tooth teens. It's not where he wants to be forever. What Scott really wants to do is write movies, big, funny movies. And he's had some luck.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>我和作家斯科特·菲弗在一起，距离一场大型电影推销会议还有两天。我们在他位于威尔希尔一栋办公楼深处的小办公室里，斯科特在电视剧《飞越比弗利》的编剧组工作，这是一部斯佩林制作的日渐衰落的电视节目，讲的是一群越来越老的青少年。这不是他想永远待的地方。斯科特真正想做的是写电影，那种大而有趣的电影。他运气还不错。

00:03:45.540 --> 00:04:07.260
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Four years ago, he sold a pitch to Columbia-- a kind of Lysistrata, but with football widows-- and was paid to write the script. Although it's now in turnaround, hiatus, limbo, no more money will be forthcoming, et cetera. Scott knows he's a good pitcher and writer and that he has a really funny, new movie idea, which he's pitched, so far, to seven producers.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>四年前，他向哥伦比亚影业推销了一个构思——有点像《利西翠妲》，但是讲的是橄榄球遗孀的故事——并获得了剧本创作费。虽然现在项目处于停滞状态，处于搁置或悬而未决的尴尬境地，不会再有钱进账等等。斯科特知道他是一个很好的推销员和作家，而且他有一个非常有趣的、新的电影构思，目前为止他已经向七位制片人推销过。

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:17.570
<v Scott Fifer>In fact, every producer I've gone to has responded very positively and very excited. So that gets me excited. And I think, "Wow, this is going to sell. I'm going to sell this pitch. I'm going to be rich."
<v Scott Fifer>事实上，我接触的每一位制片人都反应非常积极，非常兴奋。这让我很兴奋。我觉得，“哇，这肯定能卖出去。我会卖掉这个构思。我会发财的。”

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:20.480
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>So what happened with those seven so far?
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>那么那七个构思后来怎么样了？

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:27.570
<v Scott Fifer>They didn't sell, and I'm not rich.
<v Scott Fifer>都没卖出去，我也没有发财。

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:41.250
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Friday is pitch number eight. In the movie industry, different salesmen have different styles. Scott approaches the pitch like a performance piece. He acts out the whole movie, goes into different character voices, loads on a lot of detail.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>周五是第八次推销。在电影行业，不同的推销员有不同的风格。斯科特把推销当作表演。他把整部电影演出来，模拟不同的角色声音，添加大量的细节。

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:53.830
<v Scott Fifer>I am a little worried that I'm giving them too much information. My pitches tend to be a little long. They go about 20 minutes. And I know other people that pitch just the trailer to the movie.
<v Scott Fifer>我有点担心我给他们的信息太多了。我的推销通常有点长。大约20分钟。我知道其他人只推销电影的预告片。

00:04:53.830 --> 00:05:11.300
<v Scott Fifer>I was talking to Andrew Marlowe, who did Air Force One. And when he goes into a pitch, he's just like, "Arnold Schwarzenegger coming back from the dead and meeting Satan. And he turns to the camera and says, 'I'll meet you in hell.'" And that's all he has to do when he sells a million dollar pitch.
<v Scott Fifer>我曾与做过《空军一号》的安德鲁·马洛交谈。当他进行推销时，他只是说：“阿诺德·施瓦辛格死而复生遇见了撒旦。他转向镜头说：‘我在地狱等你。’”他推销百万美元的剧本时只需做这些。

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:25.230
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>What's the secret to a million dollar pitch? Before we head out to the big meeting, for a quick primer on pitching theory, I turn to veteran pitcher Bob Kosberg, reputed king of the one-line pitch, currently a producer at Merv Griffin Productions.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>百万美元推销的秘诀是什么？在我们前往那场大会议之前，为了快速了解推销理论，我请教了资深推销员鲍勃·科斯伯格，他被誉为“单句推销之王”，目前是默夫·格里芬制作公司的制片人。

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:52.910
<v Bob Kosberg>I don't know that they're great or that they're legendary. But I can give you examples of pitches that we've sold that had those one-line, two-line hooks to them or premises that were very high concept that when an executive heard it, they right away got the idea. We sold a project called Man's Best Friend, which was really about a dog that had been genetically enhanced and then went out of control. But the key line that sold it was to then say, "In other words, Mr. Executive, it's basically Jaws on paws."
<v Bob Kosberg>我不知道它们是否伟大或传奇。但我可以给你们举一些我们卖掉的推销案例，它们都有那些一两句话的钩子，或者是非常高概念的前提，当高管听到时，他们立刻就明白了。我们卖掉了一个叫《人类最好的朋友》的项目，讲的是一只被基因增强然后失控的狗。但卖掉它的关键台词是：“换句话说，高管先生，这基本上就是《大白鲨》装了爪子。”

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:57.890
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>And then the Jaws with paws doesn't make it a better movie, but it makes the executive remember it better?
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>那么“装了爪子的《大白鲨》”并没有让电影变得更好，但让高管更容易记住了？

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:23.940
<v Bob Kosberg>It makes the executive remember it. It's a cute, little, glib sales tool that will allow them to take your pitch and go down the hall, remember as much of the pitch as they can. But then in the middle of while they're forgetting it, they can say, "But I remember the poster. It's sort of Jaws on paws." Then they can get the laugh. It's like telling a good joke. The joke travels well down the executive corridors.
<v Bob Kosberg>这让高管记住了它。这是一种可爱、简短、油嘴滑舌的销售工具，让他们可以拿着你的推销构思沿着大厅走，尽可能多地记住推销内容。但是当他们记不全的时候，他们可以说：“但我记得那个海报，它有点像装了爪子的《大白鲨》。”然后他们就能博得一笑。就像讲一个好笑话。笑话在行政走廊里传得很快。

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:42.280
<v Bob Kosberg>We sold a story called Busby to Turner. That was a fun pitch because we actually brought a dog. Everyone knows the Taco Bell chihuahua. Well, a few years ago, there was a dog that was very hot from another movie. At this point, I'm trying to remember which dog it even was. I think it was the dog from As Good As It Gets.
<v Bob Kosberg>我们向特纳电视台卖了一个叫《巴斯比》的故事。那是一次有趣的推销，因为我们真的带了一只狗。每个人都知道塔可钟的吉娃娃。嗯，几年前，有一只狗在另一部电影中非常火。此刻，我正试图回忆那到底是哪只狗。我想是《尽善尽美》里的那只狗。

00:06:42.280 --> 00:07:01.340
<v Bob Kosberg>But we took that dog around to the studios to pitch meetings. And the dog would sit at attention with a pencil in his mouth like he was ready to take notes. But ultimately, the pitch was the story-- Busby was the story of two guys who wanted to steal the world's smartest dog right off of a studio lot. "The dog was working at Paramount," we said, "making a movie called Bite Hard III."
<v Bob Kosberg>但我们带着那只狗去制片厂参加推销会议。那只狗会坐着，嘴里叼着铅笔，好像准备做笔记一样。但最终，推销的是故事——《巴斯比》的故事是关于两个想从制片厂里偷走世界上最聪明的狗的家伙。“那只狗在派拉蒙工作，”我们说，“正在拍一部叫《重咬三》的电影。”

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:21.970
<v Bob Kosberg>Well, once we got to the point in the story where we could say, "These kidnappers break on to the studio lot, steal the dog, and bring it back, and wait for the phone to ring," we could stop the pitch and say, "Basically, it's Ruthless People with a dog." And it helped that the dog was in the room. So sometimes, I'm not above cheap stunts.
<v Bob Kosberg>好吧，当我们讲到故事中可以说“这两个绑匪闯入制片厂，偷走了狗，把它带回来，等待电话响”的时候，我们就可以停下来推销说：“基本上，这就是带狗版的《小迷糊闯七关》。”而且狗在房间里也有帮助。所以有时候，我也会用一些廉价的特技。

00:07:21.970 --> 00:08:09.990
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>As Kosberg goes on to describe other legendary pitches he has sold-- Norma Rae with long legs, The Lust Boat, a Bette Midler fertility clinic mix-up caper called Scrambled Eggs-- I realize that, paradoxically, I'm much more entertained by the pitches than, well, I'd probably be by the actual movies they'd spawned. Think about it. While the phrase " Jaws with paws" is a laugh, the 90-minute hilarious summer movie version of Jaws with paws with a car chase, and a teen romance, and a wacky cameo by the comedian Sinbad sounds a little painful, which makes me wonder. Have we come to a point in Hollywood history when the pitches have become better than the movies? When the pitches themselves are somehow a purer form of movie? Bob Kosberg says, "Sure. And why not?"
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>当科斯伯格继续描述他卖掉的其他传奇推销——长腿版《诺玛·蕾》、《欲望之船》、《碎蛋》（一部贝特·米德勒主演的关于生育诊所弄错孩子的闹剧）——我意识到，矛盾的是，比起这些构思可能催生的电影，我对推销本身更感兴趣。想一想。虽然“装了爪子的《大白鲨》”是个笑话，但如果真拍成90分钟的《大白鲨》欢乐夏日电影，加上飞车追逐、青春恋情，还有喜剧演员辛巴达的客串，听起来就有点痛苦，这让我怀疑。我们是否已经到了好莱坞历史上推销比电影本身更好的地步？推销本身是否成了某种更纯粹的电影形式？鲍勃·科斯伯格说：“当然。为什么不呢？”

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:26.880
<v Bob Kosberg>Well, that's one of the wonderful things about pitching. When you're pitching the story in the first meetings, everybody visualizes the movie they want to see. It can only be the best possible version at that moment. It can only get worse from that point on because then you have to write the darn thing and directors come in with their version.
<v Bob Kosberg>嗯，这是推销的美妙之处之一。当你在第一次会议中推销故事时，每个人都会想象他们想看的电影。在那一刻，它只能是最好的版本。从那时起它只会变得更糟，因为你必须把它写出来，导演也会带着他们的版本加入。

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:42.340
<v Bob Kosberg>And now, of course, to give credit to all the talented people of Hollywood, sometimes they take a pitch, and the writer, the director, the stars, the studio make it better. But you're right. A lot of times, when you're just pitching, what's great about that stage of the development process is it's all about potential.
<v Bob Kosberg>当然，现在为了肯定好莱坞所有有才华的人，有时他们会采纳一个构思，然后作家、导演、明星、制片厂会让它变得更好。但你说得对。很多时候，当你只是在推销时，开发过程中那个阶段的伟大之处在于它全是关于潜力。

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:50.290
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Scott Fifer-- and remember that his brilliant Lysistrata football-widows pitch is now lost in turnaround limbo-- couldn't agree more.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>斯科特·菲弗——请记住他那绝妙的《利西翠妲》橄榄球遗孀推销构思现在正迷失在停滞的尴尬境地——对此深表赞同。

00:08:50.290 --> 00:09:03.170
<v Scott Fifer>That may very well be true. It's sad, but true that the pitch is as good as it's going to get. And it's just downhill from there. You keep getting notes on how to change it, and why it should be different, and all these other people start adding their opinions.
<v Scott Fifer>那可能确实是真的。很悲哀，但推销确实是它能达到的最好水平了。从那以后就是走下坡路。你会不断收到关于如何修改它的建议，为什么它应该有所不同，以及所有这些其他人开始加入他们的意见。

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:19.460
<v Scott Fifer>And it's frustrating that I still can think of that pitch and think, "My god, that's such a great idea. That's such a movie. I can see it. It stars Julia Roberts. I love it." And it may never happen. And that's so frustrating.
<v Scott Fifer>最令人沮丧的是，我依然能想起那个推销，觉得“天哪，那真是个绝妙的主意。那真是一部电影。我可以预见它。由朱莉娅·罗伯茨主演。我爱死它了。”但它可能永远不会发生。这太让人沮丧了。

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:50.860
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>To avoid frustration, Scott knows that the producers he's pitching to Friday are looking specifically for a Liar Liar type comedy, which is the very type of movie he's pitching. Although, depending on his audience, he can also pitch it as a Dumb And Dumber comedy or a Nutty Professor comedy. How will he know his pitch is succeeding? When the executives laugh. Who is he pitching to? Two successful and in-demand producers, whose most recent movie is being released by Dreamworks, Dan Jinks and Bruce Cohen.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>为了避免沮丧，斯科特知道周五他要向其推销的制片人正在专门寻找那种《大话王》风格的喜剧，这正是他正在推销的电影类型。虽然根据观众的不同，他也可以将其推销为《阿呆与阿瓜》喜剧或《肥佬教授》喜剧。他怎么知道他的推销成功了？当高管们笑的时候。他在向谁推销？两位成功且炙手可热的制片人，他们最近的电影正由梦工厂发行，丹·金克斯和布鲁斯·科恩。

00:09:50.860 --> 00:10:10.550
<v Scott Fifer>I am sure that I met these people once. But sometimes, they might not remember me because they meet more writers than I meet producers. It helps if you know the people because it is much less comfortable when you're pitching to a complete stranger. So it would be nice if they actually have some recognition when they look in my eyes.
<v Scott Fifer>我确定我见过这些人一次。但有时，他们可能不记得我，因为他们见的作家比我见的制片人多。如果你认识这些人会有所帮助，因为当你向完全陌生的人推销时，会很不自在。所以如果他们看着我的眼睛时真的能认出我，那会很好。

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:11.050
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>They don't.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>他们没有。

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:11.790
<v Scott Fifer>Hey. Scott.
<v Scott Fifer>嘿。斯科特。

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.340
<v Dan Jinks>Nice to meet you, Scott. Dan Jinks. How are you?
<v Dan Jinks>很高兴见到你，斯科特。我是丹·金克斯。你好吗？

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:14.070
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>I'm Sandra. It's nice--
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>我是桑德拉。很高兴——

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.370
<v Dan Jinks>Sandra, how are you? We've met before.
<v Dan Jinks>桑德拉，你好吗？我们以前见过。

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:16.650
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Yes, we have. We have.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>是的，我们见过。确实见过。

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:21.270
<v Dan Jinks>I don't think I've never met before. But I read you. You wrote Starstruck?
<v Dan Jinks>我不认为我见过。但我读过你的作品。你写了《星光熠熠》？

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:25.310
<v Scott Fifer>Yes. Though actually, we have met. But that's OK.
<v Scott Fifer>是的。虽然实际上，我们见过面。不过没关系。

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:25.990
<v Dan Jinks>I couldn't remember.
<v Dan Jinks>我记不清了。

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:33.900
<v Scott Fifer>There's a lot of mes, and not a lot of yous. I came to your house up in the Hollywood Hills.
<v Scott Fifer>有很多个像我这样的人，而像你们这样的人不多。我曾去过你们在好莱坞山的房子。

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:34.780
<v Dan Jinks>That's right.
<v Dan Jinks>那是真的。

00:10:34.780 --> 00:11:11.600
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Now let me cue you in to what's physically happening. Dan Jinks and Bruce Cohen, the producers, are settling themselves into chairs on the right side of the room, which is fairly bare. These are relatively new offices, ones they've just moved into. Scott, looking subtly natty, and yet casual, in black, button-down DKNY shirt and black jeans, is settling himself into a chair on the left. What's extraordinary, given the clear division between seller and buyer-- I mean Scott is here to pitch an idea which Dan and Bruce will either buy or reject. It'll be over as soon as he leaves the room, possibly even before he leaves the room.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>现在让我告诉你发生了什么。制片人丹·金克斯和布鲁斯·科恩在房间右侧的椅子上坐下，房间里相当空。这些是相对较新的办公室，他们刚搬进来。斯科特穿着黑色的DKNY纽扣衬衫和黑色牛仔裤，显得微妙而时髦，又很随性，在左侧的椅子上坐下。鉴于买卖双方之间明显的界限——我的意思是斯科特在这里推销一个丹和布鲁斯要么买要么拒绝的构思——这非常非同寻常。他一离开房间这一切就结束了，甚至可能在他离开房间之前就结束了。

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:46.330
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Anyway, what's extraordinary, given that there are literally hundreds of thousands of dollars at stake, is the intense sense of casualness, openness, even democracy, in the room. Everyone's 30-ish or so. No one's in a suit. It's as if the stakes are so frighteningly high that the only way anyone could get through this is if everyone does their best to pretend nothing's at stake. And so the producers are bantering with us. And, more importantly, they are practicing that peculiar playing-field-leveling gambit you might think of as "The ritual of the water."
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>无论如何，考虑到实际上有成千上万的美元悬而未决，最非同寻常的是房间里那种强烈的随意感、开放感，甚至是民主。每个人都在30岁左右。没有人穿西装。就好像赌注高得令人恐惧，以至于任何人能挺过这一关的唯一方法就是每个人都尽最大努力假装没有任何赌注。所以制片人们在和我们开玩笑。更重要的是，他们正在练习那种你们可以称之为“水的仪式”的独特的拉平竞争环境的策略。

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:49.280
<v Dan Jinks>How's everyone? Do you want waters?
<v Dan Jinks>大家怎么样？要喝水吗？

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:50.710
<v Scott Fifer>I would like some, please.
<v Scott Fifer>我想要一点，谢谢。

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.010
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Yeah.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>好的。

00:11:52.010 --> 00:12:02.870
<v Dan Jinks>Can I tell you the exciting thing is that we were renting furniture here. And now we've bought furniture, but we don't have a couch yet, so this is why-- oh, who didn't get a water? You didn't get a water. Anyway, so--
<v Dan Jinks>我可以告诉你一件令人兴奋的事，我们之前一直在这里租家具。现在我们买了家具，但还没有沙发，所以这就是为什么——噢，谁没拿到水？你没拿到水。总之，所以——

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:18.950
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>In a nutshell, here's what the plastic bottled water, traveling hand to hand, from producer-- buyer-- to writer-- seller-- tends to mean. And the water always travels buyer to seller. Do writers ever bring producers bottles of water when they pitch? No.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>简而言之，这种从制片人（买方）传到作家（卖方）手中的塑料瓶装水往往意味着什么。而且水总是从买方传向卖方。作家在推销时会给制片人带瓶装水吗？不会。

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:53.100
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Anyway, in my personal experience, the water is a courtesy gesture that always implies a lack of something. At the beginning of a meeting, "We don't know you. Have a water." In the middle of a meeting, "Leslie Ann here, may be nothing more than a poorly-paid intern, but even she deserves a water." At the end of a meeting for an unsuccessful pitch, "In lieu of actual money, here, take an extra water." At this moment, rolling around on the floor of my car, are literally about a dozen half-filled plastic bottles of water.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>总之，根据我的个人经验，水是一种礼貌的姿态，总是暗示着某种东西的缺失。会议开始时，“我们不认识你。喝点水吧。”会议中间，“这边的莱斯利·安可能只是一个薪水微薄的实习生，但即使是她也应该有一瓶水。”会议结束时，对于推销不成功的会议，“没钱给，给你瓶水吧。”此刻，我的车地板上翻滚着大概十二瓶喝了一半的塑料瓶装水。

00:12:53.100 --> 00:13:06.560
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>What the water is, also, is a banter helper. The pre-pitch banter, of course, being another key democratizing ritual. "How was your drive over? Did you find our office OK?" "Yes, I did. But that left turn onto Santa Monica, I hate that turn."
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>水也是一种玩笑助手。推销前的寒暄当然是另一种关键的民主化仪式。“开车过来怎么样？找到我们办公室了吗？”“是的，找到了。但是圣莫尼卡那个左转，我讨厌那个转弯。”

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:36.620
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>The four basic modes of executive banter are, one, how was your drive over? Two, we know some of the same people. Three, I was just reviewing your work, which I followed for many years, and thinking how much I especially loved your piece on-- you'd be surprised how rarely that happens. And finally, of course, there's the dreaded four. "Sorry you had to wait for 40 minutes. It's just that my son's 10th birthday party is happening on Saturday, and the whole household is in an uproar."
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>高管寒暄的四种基本模式是：一，开车过来怎么样？二，我们认识一些共同的朋友。三，我刚审阅了你的作品，我已经关注很多年了，想到我特别喜欢你关于……你可能会惊讶这种事情很少发生。最后，当然还有令人畏惧的第四种。“抱歉让你等了40分钟。只是因为我儿子的10岁生日聚会要在周六举行，家里乱成一团。”

00:13:36.620 --> 00:14:09.200
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>In general, the actual content of the banter is less important than how long it lasts, which is always a delicate matter. As the pitcher, you don't necessarily want to be the first to cut the banter off, abruptly transitioning a warm, us-focused human room into a crude, me-focused "Have I got something to sell you" room. Also, if an executive encourages a long period of banter, it can be a good thing because it means "I'm loving this conversation. I'm loving you. Eddie Murphy enjoys working with down-to-earth people. Let's make a deal."
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>通常，寒暄的实际内容不如它持续的时间重要，这总是一个微妙的问题。作为推销员，你并不想成为第一个突兀地切断寒暄的人，将一个温暖的、以我们为中心的人性化房间变成一个粗鲁的、以我为中心的“我有东西要卖给你”的房间。此外，如果一位高管鼓励长时间的寒暄，这可能是一件好事，因为它意味着“我喜欢这次谈话。我喜欢你。艾迪·墨菲喜欢和脚踏实地的人一起工作。让我们达成协议吧。”

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:57.290
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>On the other hand, it can go on too long. I once went to a pitch meeting at a major television network, where the executive told hilarious stories about herself for 45 minutes out of a 1 hour meeting, with apparently huge enjoyment, eliciting regular, rhythmic howls of laughter from her sub-executives. She had literally talked so long, I'd forgotten why I was there or what I was supposed to be pitching. All I was aware of was of my body tossing forward and back with the others, slaves rowing together in a kind of merciless comedy galley of hilarity. Perhaps you've noticed the hilarious TV sitcom Suddenly Sandra wasn't on last fall's schedule.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>另一方面，它可能持续太久。我曾经去过一家主要电视台的推销会议，那位高管在一小时的会议中讲了45分钟关于她自己的搞笑故事，显然非常享受，并不时引起她的下属高管阵阵节奏性的笑声。她讲得实在太久，以至于我忘了我为什么来这里，或者我应该推销什么。我只意识到我的身体随着其他人前后晃动，就像在一种无情的喜剧划船机里一起划船的奴隶。也许你注意到了搞笑的情景喜剧《桑德拉突发事件》去年秋天没有上档。

00:14:57.290 --> 00:15:06.570
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Scott's transition from banter into the very act of selling is perfectly smooth. He unobtrusively takes out a stack of his three-by-five cards and says,
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>斯科特从寒暄过渡到销售行为的过程非常顺畅。他不动声色地拿出那叠3x5卡片说：

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:07.280
<v Scott Fifer>Anyway.
<v Scott Fifer>总之。

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:07.810
<v Dan Jinks>Anyway.
<v Dan Jinks>总之。

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:09.010
<v Scott Fifer>So I have this pitch for you.
<v Scott Fifer>所以我有一个构思要推销给你们。

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:09.370
<v Dan Jinks>Yes.
<v Dan Jinks>好的。

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:10.400
<v Bruce Cohen>Wonderful.
<v Bruce Cohen>太棒了。

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:12.280
<v Scott Fifer>And I don't know if you know too much about it.
<v Scott Fifer>我不知道你们对它了解多少。

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:14.980
<v Bruce Cohen>I don't think I have any idea what it is. So I'm excited.
<v Bruce Cohen>我想我完全不知道是什么。所以我很兴奋。

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:23.440
<v Scott Fifer>All right. Well, I'll tell you it's a comedy. And that it's in the vein of Liar Liar. I used that as my role model because I figured, that's a great way to go.
<v Scott Fifer>好的。嗯，我告诉你们，这是一个喜剧。它和《大话王》是一个路数的。我用它作为我的榜样，因为我想，这是一个很好的方向。

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:24.380
<v Dan Jinks>I like it a lot actually.
<v Dan Jinks>我其实非常喜欢。

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:27.320
<v Scott Fifer>Exactly. So if you're ready.
<v Scott Fifer>正是如此。所以如果你准备好了。

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:28.110
<v Dan Jinks>We're ready.
<v Dan Jinks>我们准备好了。

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:45.150
<v Scott Fifer>Our hero is TC-- Jim Carrey, Eddie Murphy, Adam Sandler-- he runs a Mrs. Goody's. Think of Mrs. Field's store. And he hates it. He hates rolling the dough, hates baking the cookies. He hates being nice to his picky customers we see. And he hates the sappy logo, which is a saccharine photo of Mrs. Goody herself, TC's mother.
<v Scott Fifer>我们的主人公是TC——像吉姆·凯瑞、艾迪·墨菲、亚当·桑德勒那样的人——他经营一家“古迪太太”店。想象一下“菲尔德太太”那种店。但他讨厌它。他讨厌揉面团，讨厌烤饼干。他讨厌对那些挑剔的顾客表现得很好。他讨厌那个感情用事的标志，那是TC的母亲、“古迪太太”本人的一张甜腻的照片。

00:15:45.150 --> 00:16:03.850
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>The premise Scott is moving toward, what's calculated to strike the listener at about minute six or seven of the pitch, is what if all the sayings your mother ever said to you came true? An apple a day keeps the doctor away. Frown in your face will stay that way. Swallow a watermelon seed, and a watermelon will grow in your stomach, et cetera.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>斯科特正在推进的前提，也就是旨在在推销进行到第六或第七分钟时打动听众的是，如果你的母亲对你说的所有话都成真了会怎样？一天一个苹果，医生远离我。皱眉头脸就会一直那样。吞下西瓜籽，胃里就会长出西瓜，等等。

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:16.500
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>It's commercial. It's funny. It's well worked out. And yet, as Scott pitches on, exuding confidence, calmly flipping his index cards, I remember a thing he'd said to me on Wednesday.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>它很有商业价值。它很有趣。它构思得很好。然而，当斯科特继续推销，流露出自信，冷静地翻动他的索引卡片时，我记得他在周三对我说过的话。

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:40.370
<v Scott Fifer>When they're not laughing, if there's a long gap, it probably makes me talk faster. Because then I think, "All right, I'm losing them. I've got to speed up here and get to the next funny point." So if I even speed up and talk faster than I was before, which is almost impossible, then you know I think I'm in trouble. And I'm just trying to get to something even funnier. And I'm flipping through the cards like, "When's the next funny thing?"
<v Scott Fifer>当他们不笑的时候，如果有一段很长的空白，可能会让我说得更快。因为那时我想，“好吧，我失去他们了。我得加快速度，讲到下一个笑点。”所以如果我甚至比以前说得更快，这几乎是不可能的，那你就会知道我觉得自己有麻烦了。我只是想讲点更有趣的事情。我翻着卡片想着：“下一个笑点什么时候到？”

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:49.330
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>And now, midway through the pitch, I realize Scott is literally talking faster than I've ever heard him-- or really, any other living human being-- talk before.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>现在，推销进行到一半，我意识到斯科特的语速简直是我听过他——或者任何其他活人——说话最快的一次。

00:16:49.330 --> 00:17:00.230
<v Scott Fifer>TC's completely stuffed, but he has to eat it all because of the starving kids in Africa. So now he's comically gobbling down food. He doesn't want it. It's not the romantic evening he planned at all. Now the CEO is always--
<v Scott Fifer>TC完全吃撑了，但他必须把它全部吃掉，因为非洲有挨饿的孩子。所以现在他滑稽地狼吞虎咽地吃着食物。他根本不想吃。这根本不是他计划中的浪漫夜晚。现在首席执行官总是——

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:21.440
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Throughout all this, Bruce occasionally laughs. But Dan makes no sound at all. He just sits there, smiling. Is it a warm smile? A civil smile? I can't tell. Around minute 13 though, when Scott gets to the big masturbation joke, he clearly starts to win them over. And, to my surprise and relief, the ending of the pitch is warm.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>在整个过程中，布鲁斯偶尔会笑。但丹一声不吭。他只是坐在那儿，微笑着。这是温暖的微笑吗？礼貌的微笑？我看不出来。不过在大约第13分钟，当斯科特讲到那个关于手淫的大笑话时，他显然开始赢得他们了。而且，让我惊讶和宽慰的是，推销的结尾很温暖。

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:29.560
<v Scott Fifer>But he gives him a long, passionate kiss to hold him over. And he thanks her and asks her if she has any carrots because he's going to need them in the morning. The end.
<v Scott Fifer>但他给了她一个长长而热情的吻来安慰她。他感谢她，并问她是否有胡萝卜，因为他明天早上需要它们。结束。

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:29.920
<v Bruce Cohen>Bravo.
<v Bruce Cohen>太棒了。

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:34.460
<v Dan Jinks>There you go.
<v Dan Jinks>就是这样。

00:17:34.460 --> 00:17:36.360
<v Scott Fifer>Can I have some water?
<v Scott Fifer>我能喝点水吗？

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:41.990
<v Dan Jinks>I will tell you my thoughts. Are you open to hearing thoughts and suggestions about it?
<v Dan Jinks>我会告诉你我的想法。你愿意听听关于它的想法和建议吗？

00:17:41.990 --> 00:17:43.100
<v Scott Fifer>Of course.
<v Scott Fifer>当然。

00:17:43.100 --> 00:18:07.800
<v Dan Jinks>I really like it a lot. I had a couple of thoughts. Right now, the way you describe this guy is he seems like he's almost too much of a schmuck. It's hard for us, as an audience, to care about what happens to him. And I think that to make this movie work better, I think that we need to have an investment in his story and in him.
<v Dan Jinks>我真的很喜欢它。我有几个想法。现在，你描述这个家伙的方式，他看起来几乎太像个笨蛋了。作为观众，我们很难关心他身上会发生什么。我认为为了让这部电影效果更好，我们需要对他的故事和他本人有所投入。

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:18.180
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Dan and Bruce critique the pitch for 10 minutes, an unusually long time. And it's hard to know how to read this. Does this mean they're interested? Or that they hate it?
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>丹和布鲁斯对这个构思进行了10分钟的批评，时间异常长。很难判断如何解读这个。这是否意味着他们感兴趣？还是他们讨厌它？

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:30.410
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>And then, at the end, their final question is, "Have our notes made sense to you?" They don't promise they'll call or consult with others in their production company. This does not seem to bode well.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>然后，最后，他们的问题是，“我们的建议对你有意义吗？”他们没有承诺会打电话或咨询制作公司的其他人。这看起来并不乐观。

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:31.260
<v Scott Fifer>Well, thanks for listening.
<v Scott Fifer>好吧，谢谢你们的倾听。

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.320
<v Bruce Cohen>Thank you for--
<v Bruce Cohen>谢谢你——

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:35.000
<v Dan Jinks>Thanks for coming in and telling  it to us.
<v Dan Jinks>谢谢你过来告诉我们。

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:48.760
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Now the only thing that remains is the long walk to the elevator. Before we leave the building, I make Scott duck into the only private place I can think of, the ladies lounge, for a quick post-mortem.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>现在唯一剩下的就是去电梯的长路。在我们离开大楼之前，我让斯科特溜进我能想到的唯一私人地方——女士休息室，进行快速的总结。

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:54.610
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>So how do you feel the pitch went from your side? Obviously, it went great. But what was the experience like for you?
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>那么你觉得你的推销进行得怎么样？很明显，进行得很棒。但对你来说体验如何？

00:18:54.610 --> 00:19:03.530
<v Scott Fifer>I felt it went well. I could tell that they were into it. They were paying attention. I didn't feel like it was the greatest pitch, but I could tell it was going well.
<v Scott Fifer>我觉得进行得很顺利。我能看出他们很投入。他们很专注。我觉得这不算是最棒的推销，但我能看出进行得很顺利。

00:19:03.530 --> 00:19:06.990
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Into the ladies lounge suddenly appears a lady.
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>女士休息室里突然出现了一位女士。

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:13.520
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>I'm sorry. We're just finishing an interview. Go through. Sorry. So any other thoughts about how it went?
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>对不起。我们刚结束采访。请过。抱歉。那么关于推销情况还有什么想法吗？

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:27.210
<v Scott Fifer>No, they gave a lot of notes, which usually-- I'm surprised they gave so many notes. That was more than I expected and, sometimes, more than you want. Because you're just like, "All right. You know what? I just did it. I'm done here. You decide. Let me go home. I don't need to hear your philosophies on filmmaking."
<v Scott Fifer>没有，他们给了很多建议，通常——我很惊讶他们给了这么多建议。这比我预期的要多，有时，比你想要的还要多。因为你会想，“好吧。你知道吗？我刚做完了。我在这里结束了。你们决定吧。让我回家吧。我不需要听你们关于电影制作的哲学。”

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:46.100
<v Scott Fifer>But their notes were good. I think they're just tweaking notes. And they were just little things. They weren't about major-- I think it maybe a matter of them, how they perceive some things. Or maybe I spoke too fast. And just hitting different points and emphasizing. So their notes were fine. And I agreed with them.
<v Scott Fifer>但他们的建议很好。我认为他们只是微调建议。而且只是小事情。它们不是关于重大的——我想这可能是他们感知某些事物的方式问题。或者也许我说得太快了。只是触及不同的点并强调。所以他们的建议很好。我同意他们。

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:52.730
<v Sandra Tsing Loh>Scott heads to the elevator, and I walk back into the producers' offices for a quick Siskel & Ebert on Scott's performance.
