<v Radio Ad Salesman>I had this boss who had been in sales his whole life. And he taught me that when I started feeling nervous, it probably meant they were feeling nervous. And that was good. And the more good I felt about it, the more control I had over both of our nervousnesses. And, therefore, if they were just blinded by it and afraid of it and I was encouraged by it and energized by it, then I just stood a better chance of coming out the winner.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>我有个老板，他一辈子都在做销售。他教我，当我开始感到紧张时，可能意味着对方也感到紧张。那是件好事。我对它感觉越好，我就越能控制我们双方的紧张感。因此，如果他们只是被恐惧遮蔽，而我被它鼓舞、充满活力，那么我就更有机会成为赢家。

00:30:10.660 --> 00:30:22.570
<v Radio Ad Salesman>Suddenly, I'm going to these sales calls. And I can't wait. I'm running to these sales calls. I'm clicking the elevator button to make it come faster because I can't wait. Because-- it's all sorts of things.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>突然间，我要去参加这些销售电话会议。我迫不及待了。我飞奔着去参加这些销售会议。我飞快地按电梯按钮让它快点来，因为我等不及了。因为——这是各种各样的事情。

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:44.730
<v Radio Ad Salesman>It's a feeling of power. It's really fun to go into a meeting with three people who are much older than you, who make a lot more money than you, who have a lot more power in the world than you, and you know that you're going to be the guy in the room who's totally calm, and they're going to be really nervous. And I love that. I love just being the cool guy, being the calm guy.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>这是一种力量感。走进一个会议室，面对三个比你年长得多、比你挣钱多得多、在世界上比你权力大得多的人，这真的很有趣，而且你知道你将成为房间里那个完全冷静的人，而他们会非常紧张。我喜欢那样。我喜欢做那个冷静的家伙。

00:30:44.730 --> 00:31:16.880
<v Radio Ad Salesman>And I loved creating these relationships, a type of relationship I've never had before, which is just this glib, friendly relationship based on complete empty chatter about the weather or TV. I developed this theory that those really popular TV shows, one major function is to provide conversation to Americans. And not conversation about the show, but the actual conversation of the show.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>我喜欢创造这些关系，一种我从未有过的关系类型，也就是这种基于关于天气或电视的完全空洞闲聊的油嘴滑舌、友好的关系。我提出了一个理论，那些非常受欢迎的电视节目，一个主要功能是为美国人提供谈话资料。不是关于节目的谈话，而是节目本身的实际谈话。

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:40.960
<v Radio Ad Salesman>You would just say, "Hey, do you remember last night when Kramer said that? Boy, that was funny. And remember when Elaine said this to Jerry? Oh, that was funny." I didn't know that people did that. People would do that to me. And then the next sales call, I'd do that to the next person. And they seemed to like it.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>你只会说，“嘿，你记得昨晚克莱默说的话吗？天哪，那太搞笑了。还记得伊莱恩对杰瑞说的话吗？噢，那太搞笑了。”我不知道人们会那样做。人们会对我那样做。然后在下一次销售会议上，我会对下一个人那样做。他们似乎很喜欢。

00:31:40.960 --> 00:32:09.490
<v Radio Ad Salesman>I started realizing it doesn't matter what you say, it doesn't matter how vapid it is. All that mattered was that I felt comfortable and that I gave them the illusion of some kind of false intimacy. At first, I found it really great, really exciting and freeing.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>我开始意识到你说什么并不重要，它有多无聊并不重要。唯一重要的是我感到舒服，而且我给了他们某种虚假亲密感的错觉。起初，我发现这真的很棒，真的很令人兴奋和解放。

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:23.920
<v Radio Ad Salesman>It kind of amazed me. First of all, how quickly I just became this different person and how much I enjoyed it. I found I would do things that I hadn't done before. I would hold doors open for women. And I would wait on an elevator until all the women had gotten off.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>这让我有点惊讶。首先，我如何迅速地变成了这个不同的人，以及我有多享受它。我发现我会做以前没做过的事情。我会为女士们扶门。我会等电梯直到所有女士都出来。

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:47.130
<v Radio Ad Salesman>Or I would compliment people on their-- if a friend made a joke, I'd say, "That's very funny. That's a very amusing joke. It reminds me of a joke I once heard." And I'd use my hands in these ways that I had learned made people feel at ease, never folded over my chest, but open and inviting.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>或者我会称赞人们——如果朋友讲了个笑话，我会说，“那太搞笑了。那是一个非常有趣的笑话。它让我想起我曾经听过的一个笑话。”我会用我学到的方式使用我的手，让人们感到放松，永远不要交叉在胸前，而是开放和邀请的姿态。

00:32:47.130 --> 00:33:04.930
<v Radio Ad Salesman>And my whole voice would change. My whole affect would change to the point where, when I would hang out with my friends, they'd make fun of me. "Why are you so formal? Why are you so fake?"
<v Radio Ad Salesman>我的整个声音都会改变。我的整个情感流露会改变，以至于当我和朋友出去玩时，他们会取笑我。“你为什么这么拘谨？你为什么这么假？”

00:33:04.930 --> 00:33:11.100
<v Ira Glass>When your friends would say these things about how you were changing, were you in any way alarmed?
<v Ira Glass>当你的朋友说这些关于你是如何改变的话时，你感到惊慌了吗？

00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:47.890
<v Radio Ad Salesman>No, I loved it. I felt like I had superpowers. I could go anywhere and talk to whoever I wanted and not give a [BLEEP]. I had always had trouble with women. I'd always had a hard time asking them out. And suddenly, I was asking out anyone. It didn't matter to me. But I just felt like I could put on this persona, and, at least in my mind, it was smooth. And people liked that person. And I liked it.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>不，我爱死它了。我觉得我有超能力。我可以去任何地方，和我想交谈的任何人交谈，而且完全不在乎。我以前和女性相处总是有麻烦。我一直很难开口约她们。突然间，我在约任何人。对我来说没区别。但我只是觉得我可以戴上这个人设，至少在我心里，这很顺畅。人们喜欢那个人。我也喜欢。

00:33:47.890 --> 00:34:04.740
<v Radio Ad Salesman>There's something essentially optimistic about being a salesman. It seems very American, very forward thinking. A no is an opportunity. It's an opportunity to learn why you got that no, what went wrong there, how you can do better.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>作为一名推销员，本质上有一种乐观主义。这看起来非常美国化，非常有前瞻性。一个“不”就是一个机会。这是一个了解为什么你得到那个“不”、哪里出了问题、你如何能做得更好的机会。

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:06.490
<v Ira Glass>So there's this notion of self-improvement?
<v Ira Glass>所以这有一个自我提升的概念？

00:34:06.490 --> 00:34:42.660
<v Radio Ad Salesman>Yeah. There were a few times where this whole process really felt like the violation of something. It felt like I went too far. There was this guy who was president of a major company. And he wanted to buy what turned out to be a lot of money in ads. And it would mean a lot of money to me personally and a lot to the station.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>是的。有几次这个整个过程真的感觉像对某种东西的亵渎。感觉我走得太远了。有个家伙是一家大公司的总裁。他想买后来证明是一大笔广告费的东西。这对我个人和电台来说意味着一大笔钱。

00:34:42.660 --> 00:34:57.280
<v Radio Ad Salesman>And it was a big decision for him because, even though he was the president, he was trying to establish his own base within the company. And they didn't want him to be on this particular station. They didn't think he should be.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>对他来说这是一个重大决定，因为即使他是总裁，他也在努力在公司内建立自己的基础。他们不想让他上这个特定的电台。他们认为他不应该上。

00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:24.780
<v Radio Ad Salesman>And I felt like I had always known his company shouldn't be on our air. I knew we weren't going to sell that product. But I prepared the charts that made it seem like we would. And I gave him the ammunition and the encouragement to fight the good fight. And he was really fighting for it.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>我觉得我一直都知道他的公司不应该在我们的频道。我知道我们不会卖掉那个产品。但我准备了图表，让它看起来好像我们会卖。我给了他武器和鼓励去打这场好仗。他真的在为它而战。

00:35:24.780 --> 00:36:17.920
<v Radio Ad Salesman>There's this one moment where he and I went out for drinks at this private men's club, which was another part of this whole ridiculous life that I had for that period of time, was belonging to a private-- not men's club-- private club. And he was really opening up. He was really talking about his wife, and their relationship, and his fears of being in this job, and whether or not he wanted to have kids, and what that would mean to him, and how he ended up being a businessman, and what other kinds of lifes he could have led, and sometimes wished he had led. And it was so awful because he really bought my false intimacy. He really saw us as friends.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>有那么一刻，我和他在这个私人男士俱乐部喝了酒，这是我那段荒唐生活的一部分，属于一个私人——不是男士俱乐部——私人俱乐部。他真的敞开了心扉。他真的谈到了他的妻子、他们的关系、他在这份工作中的恐惧、他是否想生孩子、这对意味着什么、他最终是如何成为商人的、他还可能过什么样的生活，有时希望他曾经那样做过。这太糟糕了，因为他真的买账了我的虚假亲密感。他真的把我们视为朋友。

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:48.390
<v Radio Ad Salesman>To be honest, I had had these moments before. I'd even gone to clients' homes for dinner to meet their family. I started finding that when I hit these moments of false intimacy-- there were a few stories from my childhood or a few set pieces that seemed like they were emotional sharing, but really were just set pieces that I felt very comfortable uttering. I probably said them almost word-for-word, verbatim each time. But they gave the illusion of opening up.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>老实说，我以前也有过这些时刻。我甚至去过客户家吃晚饭见他们的家人。我开始发现当我达到这些虚假亲密时刻时——有一些来自我童年的故事或一些看起来像是情感分享的固定桥段，但实际上只是我觉得说起来非常舒服的固定桥段。我可能每次都几乎逐字逐句地说出来。但它们给了人们一种敞开心扉的错觉。

00:36:48.390 --> 00:37:11.780
<v Radio Ad Salesman>And so I threw a couple of those at him. But I just knew he would never be a friend. I would never open up to him in that way, in an authentic way. And I couldn't tell him, "You know what? You shouldn't be on our air." Because he'd probably sue us. Or who knows what?
<v Radio Ad Salesman>所以我向他抛出了一些。但我只是知道他永远不会成为朋友。我永远不会以那种方式、以真实的方式对他敞开心扉。而且我不能告诉他，“你知道吗？你不应该出现在我们的频道。”因为他可能会起诉我们。或者谁知道呢？

00:37:11.780 --> 00:37:12.930
<v Ira Glass>Because he was already on your air?
<v Ira Glass>因为他已经出现在你的频道上了？

00:37:12.930 --> 00:37:29.700
<v Radio Ad Salesman>He was already on my air. And I wanted his money. I did. It meant a lot of money to me. And I couldn't tell him that I didn't want to be his friend. What would be the point of that?
<v Radio Ad Salesman>他已经出现在我的频道上了。而且我想要他的钱。我是这么想的。它对我来说意味着一大笔钱。而且我不能告诉他我不愿意做他的朋友。那有什么意义呢？

00:37:29.700 --> 00:37:43.030
<v Radio Ad Salesman>And I just remember feeling just stuck, like I didn't know-- There didn't seem to be any way to deal with him honorably.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>我只记得感觉被困住了，就像我不知道——似乎没有任何办法能体面地处理他。

00:37:43.030 --> 00:37:45.120
<v Ira Glass>So what did you do?
<v Ira Glass>那你做了什么？

00:37:45.120 --> 00:38:02.000
<v Radio Ad Salesman>I kept selling to him. And eventually, he left that company. And I never knew if he got fired or if he quit on his own. I never knew if it had anything to do with us.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>我继续向他销售。最终，他离开了那家公司。我永远不知道他是被解雇了还是自己辞职的。我永远不知道这是否与我们有关。

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:04.200
<v Ira Glass>He might have lost his job over that money.
<v Ira Glass>他可能因为那笔钱丢了工作。

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:07.990
<v Radio Ad Salesman>Yeah, it's possible. I don't know. Yeah.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>是的，有可能。我不知道。是的。

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:10.350
<v Ira Glass>And he trusted you?
<v Ira Glass>他信任你？

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:33.780
<v Radio Ad Salesman>Yeah. At the end of the day, you have to decide, look, it's their money. They're spending the money. And if they're spending the money based on the information I've given them, and only the information I've given them, then it's their fault. Shame on them for not doing the research and not seeking advisors.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>是的。在一天结束时，你必须决定，看，那是他们的钱。他们在花钱。如果他们是根据我提供的信息在花钱，而且只根据我提供的信息，那么那是他们的错。怪他们没有做研究，没有寻求建议。

00:38:33.780 --> 00:38:36.690
<v Ira Glass>Of course, that's like saying, shame on them for believing me.
<v Ira Glass>当然，这就像是说，怪他们相信我。

00:38:36.690 --> 00:38:54.460
<v Radio Ad Salesman>Right. Yeah, it is. But you say that a lot.
<v Radio Ad Salesman>对。是的，确实是。但你经常这么说。

00:38:54.460 --> 00:39:13.630
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, The Secret To Being Rich And Happy. There are usually only two stories we Americans tell ourselves about our salesmen, about our own lives as salesmen. There's the super salesman story. Picture, please, the early days of Donald Trump. And there's the tragic, down-on-his-luck salesman. Picture the man in anything by, say, David Mamet.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，“富有且快乐的秘诀”。我们美国人讲述我们自己的推销员、我们作为推销员的生活的故事通常只有两个。一个是超级推销员的故事。请想象一下唐纳德·特朗普的早年。另一个是运气不佳的悲剧推销员。请想象一下大卫·马梅特任何作品中的人。

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:37.230
<v Ira Glass>For a more accurate look at the life of an American salesman, consider, please, Jimmy Roy. "Diamond" Jimmy Roy. 76 years old. Lives and works in a town not far from Pittsburgh. He has sold everything from used cars to antiques to jewelry, all with a philosophy of sales that he has created himself, as many salesmen have to create a philosophy for themselves, to get themselves through it. Reporter Dan Collison hung around him for a few days.
<v Ira Glass>为了更准确地观察美国推销员的生活，请考虑一下吉米·罗。“钻石”吉米·罗。76岁。在离匹兹堡不远的城镇生活和工作。他卖过从二手车到古董到珠宝的所有东西，带着他自己创造的销售哲学，正如许多推销员必须为自己创造一种哲学来支撑自己一样。记者丹·科里森在他身边待了几天。

00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:49.480
<v Dan Collison>As Jimmy Roy tells it, there was a time in the 1950s and '60s when he owned the town, the town of Braddock, Pennsylvania, located just downriver from Pittsburgh. But not everyone in Braddock remembers those days.
<v Dan Collison>正如吉米·罗所说，在20世纪50年代和60年代，有一段时间他拥有那个城镇，即宾夕法尼亚州的布拉多克，就在匹兹堡下游。但并不是布拉多克的每个人都记得那些日子。

00:39:49.480 --> 00:39:50.270
<v Clifton Nelson>How you doing?
<v Clifton Nelson>你好吗？

00:39:50.270 --> 00:39:50.900
<v Jimmy Roy>You know me?
<v Jimmy Roy>你认识我吗？

00:39:50.900 --> 00:39:51.310
<v Clifton Nelson>Huh?
<v Clifton Nelson>嗯？

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:52.070
<v Jimmy Roy>You know me?
<v Jimmy Roy>你认识我吗？

00:39:52.070 --> 00:39:53.110
<v Clifton Nelson>No, I do not.
<v Clifton Nelson>不，我不认识。

00:39:53.110 --> 00:39:54.050
<v Jimmy Roy>J. Roy.
<v Jimmy Roy>J. 罗。

00:39:54.050 --> 00:39:55.180
<v Clifton Nelson>My name is Clifton Nelson.
<v Clifton Nelson>我的名字是克利夫顿·尼尔森。

00:39:55.180 --> 00:39:57.280
<v Jimmy Roy>OK. You're not from around here, are you?
<v Jimmy Roy>好的。你不是本地人，对吧？

00:39:57.280 --> 00:39:58.980
<v Clifton Nelson>Yes, I am. Clifton Lamont Nelson, Junior.
<v Clifton Nelson>是的，我是。克利夫顿·拉蒙特·尼尔森，小。

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:02.900
<v Jimmy Roy>And you don't know J. Roy that used to own all the property here?
<v Jimmy Roy>你不认识曾经拥有这里所有房产的J. 罗？

00:40:02.900 --> 00:40:03.430
<v Clifton Nelson>No, sir.
<v Clifton Nelson>不，先生。

00:40:03.430 --> 00:40:04.860
<v Jimmy Roy>Automobile business and everything?
<v Jimmy Roy>汽车生意什么的？

00:40:04.860 --> 00:40:06.000
<v Clifton Nelson>No, sir.
<v Clifton Nelson>不，先生。

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:26.680
<v Jimmy Roy>OK. Used to own a car lot over here. Owned this building here. Owned that house over here. Owned the Dillinger building, the furniture stores down here, the two furniture stores. I owned The Chicken Shack. You know The Chicken Shack on Fifth Street?
<v Jimmy Roy>好的。曾经在这儿有个车行。拥有这里的这栋楼。拥有那边的那栋房子。拥有迪林格大楼、这边的家具店、那两家家具店。我拥有“鸡肉棚”。你知道第五街的“鸡肉棚”吗？

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:29.210
<v Clifton Nelson>I think I know of it. I think I know it.
<v Clifton Nelson>我想我知道。我想我知道它。

00:40:29.210 --> 00:40:31.740
<v Jimmy Roy>And I forget what else I owned.
<v Jimmy Roy>我忘了我还拥有什么了。

00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:49.760
<v Dan Collison>Back then, Braddock was known as the "valley's greatest shopping center." On Saturdays, people would come from all over, and the sidewalks were jammed with shoppers. Then the steel mills started shutting down. People moved away. Suburban malls sprang up. And no one came to Braddock anymore.
<v Dan Collison>那时，布拉多克被称为“山谷最伟大的购物中心”。周六，人们从四面八方赶来，人行道上挤满了购物者。后来钢铁厂开始关闭。人们搬走了。郊区购物中心兴起了。再也没人来布拉多克了。

00:40:49.760 --> 00:41:01.020
<v Recording Of Jimmy Roy>[SINGING] Every time it rains, it rains pennies from heaven. Don't you know each cloud contains pennies from heaven?
<v Recording Of Jimmy Roy>[唱歌] 每当下雨时，天堂就下起便士。难道你不知道每朵云都包含着来自天堂的便士吗？

00:41:01.020 --> 00:41:25.490
<v Dan Collison>By the mid-'70s, Braddock was becoming a ghost town. It was about this time that filmmaker Tony Buba featured Jimmy Roy in one of his short documentaries on Braddock. That's Jimmy there singing. The occasion was the grand opening of J. Roy's Flea Market and New and Used Furniture Store. His other businesses had died. It was Jimmy's last stand.
<v Dan Collison>到70年代中期，布拉多克正变成一座鬼城。大约就在那时，电影制片人托尼·布巴在他关于布拉多克的短纪录片中以吉米·罗为主角。那是吉米在唱歌。当时是J. 罗跳蚤市场和新旧家具店的盛大开业。他的其他生意都死了。这是吉米的背水一战。

00:41:25.490 --> 00:41:57.150
<v Recording Of Jimmy Roy>What's so good about this building is we have a drive-in ramp, where we can hold about 50 used cars up there. And the future plan, we're going to put a drive-in used car lot, plus a drive-in used and new furniture store upstairs. The nice thing about it is people will able to drive right in, get out of their car, look at some used cars, look at antiques, look at new furniture, and look at used furniture. And I really believe that this time I've struck on something that people really need.
<v Recording Of Jimmy Roy>这栋楼最好的是我们有一个免下车坡道，我们可以在上面容纳50辆二手车。未来的计划是，我们要建立一个免下车二手车行，外加楼上的免下车二手和新家具店。好的是人们可以直接开车进来，下车，看看二手车，看看古董，看看新家具，看看二手家具。我真的相信这次我找到了人们真正需要的东西。

00:41:57.150 --> 00:42:21.380
<v Recording Of Jimmy Roy>But I think we'll have no problem here in making it go. And I believe after that, that it's going to be Easy Street. I really believe that Saturday is going to mark a big day in my life. The grand opening of the J. Roy Flea Market and New and Used Furniture.
<v Recording Of Jimmy Roy>我想我们会在这里顺利运作。我相信在那之后，这就是“轻松街”了。我真的相信周六将标志着我生命中的重要一天。J. 罗跳蚤市场和新旧家具店的盛大开业。

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:35.180
<v Dan Collison>Today, the street sign at the corner of Verona and Braddock Avenue, where the store was located, is so rusted out, it can't be read. J. Roy's Flea Market and New and Used Furniture didn't quite live up to Jimmy's expectations.
<v Dan Collison>今天，维罗纳和布拉多克大道拐角处的街道标志，也就是商店所在的地方，锈迹斑斑，已经无法辨认了。J. 罗跳蚤市场和新旧家具店并没有完全达到吉米的期望。

00:42:35.180 --> 00:43:16.860
<v Jimmy Roy>I bet everything I had. And I bet my last penny, what I could borrow, and everything else. I bet it on this town. I bought everything. I stretched myself out to the last penny. I bet on it. I had no fear. But when I saw I had to face reality, everyone started to be scared to come to the town. You can't sell people who don't come to the town. So I figured I better get out while I can get out alive. So I did.
<v Jimmy Roy>我押上了我拥有的一切。我押上了我最后一分钱，我能借到的一切，以及其他所有东西。我把一切都押在这个镇上了。我买下了一切。我伸展到了最后一分钱。我押上了它。我没有恐惧。但当我看到我必须面对现实时，每个人都开始害怕来这个镇。你不能把东西卖给不来这个镇的人。所以我认为我最好在活着的时候赶紧离开。所以我做了。

00:43:16.860 --> 00:43:31.770
<v Jimmy Roy>I go along with Shakespeare, "Nothing is good or bad, except thinking makes it so." Or the so-called words of Jesus. He says, "As a man thinketh in his heart, so is he." They musta knew something.
<v Jimmy Roy>我同意莎士比亚的观点，“事物本身没有好坏之分，全在于人们的思想。”或者耶稣那所谓的名言。他说，“人心里怎么想，他就是怎样的人。”他们一定知道些什么。

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:53.400
<v Dan Collison>These days, if you're looking for Jimmy Roy, chances are you'll find him at the Plaza Restaurant, just over the hill from Braddock. The Plaza is sort of like Jimmy's office. It's located in a 1960s strip mall next to a Shop and Save. It's the kind of family place with racks of pies in a glass case by the front door and where waitresses and their daughters work side by side.
<v Dan Collison>这些天，如果你在找吉米·罗，很有可能你会在广场餐厅找到他，就在布拉多克的小山那边。广场餐厅有点像吉米的办公室。它位于Shop and Save旁边的60年代条状购物中心。这是那种家庭式的餐馆，前门有个玻璃柜架着馅饼，女服务员和她们的女儿并肩工作。

00:43:53.400 --> 00:44:15.340
<v Dan Collison>Jimmy Roy sits in a booth in the back of the smoking section. He's dressed in a white fedora, a white linen jacket with a maroon, silk handkerchief, a colorful tie with a diamond stick pin, and black and white alligator-skin shoes. He's wearing five diamond studded gold necklaces, two gold bracelets on each wrist, and he has diamond rings on every finger.
<v Dan Collison>吉米·罗坐在吸烟区后面的一张隔间里。他戴着白色软呢帽，穿着白色亚麻外套，配着栗色丝绸手帕，系着色彩鲜艳的领带，上面别着钻石针，穿着黑白鳄鱼皮鞋。他戴着五条镶钻金项链，每个手腕上有两条金手镯，每个手指都戴着钻戒。

00:44:15.340 --> 00:44:33.420
<v Jimmy Roy>These are just some of the things I pick up when I sell, right off my hand. This is a little cluster, a men's pinkie ring. This is another cluster, index ring. They're not the most expensive, but they're diamonds. This is another cluster. And this is a emerald.
<v Jimmy Roy>这些只是我在销售时顺手买的一些东西。这是一个小簇，男士小指戒指。这是另一个簇，食指戒指。它们不是最贵的，但它们是钻石。这是另一个簇。这是一个祖母绿。

00:44:33.420 --> 00:44:42.880
<v Jimmy Roy>Yeah, that's my store now. That's the store. I operate my stores. No overhead there. I don't have to pay rent.
<v Jimmy Roy>是的，那就是我现在的商店。那就是商店。我经营我的商店。那里没有间接成本。我不用付租金。

00:44:42.880 --> 00:45:01.990
<v Dan Collison>This kind of change of fortune, going from owning virtually half of Braddock to hustling jewelry from a restaurant booth, is the sort of thing that might crush other men. Not Jimmy Roy. He's steeled himself against hardship with a philosophy of salesmanship that's more like a philosophy of life that he's been refining for decades.
<v Dan Collison>这种命运的改变，从拥有布拉多克的一半财产到在餐馆隔间里推销珠宝，可能会压垮其他人。吉米·罗不会。他用一种销售哲学使自己免受苦难，这更像是一种他已经提炼了几十年的生活哲学。

00:45:01.990 --> 00:45:21.860
<v Jimmy Roy>I think it's the mind. I'm so convinced that for 42 years of some studying of the mind, the mind is the most important thing we have. The mind is the directing force of thinking, is responsible for where you're going, whether you're going to be successful, whether you're going to be a failure.
<v Jimmy Roy>我认为这是心灵的作用。我坚信，在42年的心灵研究中，心灵是我们拥有的最重要的东西。心灵是思想的指挥力量，决定了你的去向，你是会成功，还是会失败。

00:45:21.860 --> 00:45:34.820
<v Dan Collison>To Jimmy, it's all very simple. You tell yourself who you want to be, what you want to be. And if you do these positive visualizations long enough, great things will happen to you. It goes like this.
<v Dan Collison>对吉米来说，一切都很简单。你告诉自己你想成为谁，你想成为什么。如果你进行这些积极的可视化足够长的时间，伟大的事情就会发生在你身上。它是这样进行的。

00:45:34.820 --> 00:46:29.280
<v Jimmy Roy>Early in the morning when you rise, you feed yourself great thoughts, such as concentration, peace, poise, non-resistance, achievement, vitality, strength, life, youth. You feed yourself these thoughts before you leave the house. And don't concern yourself about how these thoughts are gonna manifest the beauty that you'll receive from planting the great thoughts in your mind, no more than you would be concerned about planting a tomato seed. Once you plant it, you're unconcerned. You know you planted in nice dirt and nice ground. You know that you will receive your tomatoes. To control your thinking is to control your life, control your success.
<v Jimmy Roy>清晨起床时，你给自己注入伟大的思想，例如专注、平静、镇定、不抗拒、成就、活力、力量、生命、青春。你在离家之前给自己注入这些思想。不要担心这些思想会如何显现，正如你不会担心播下番茄种子会带来你从播下伟大思想的心灵中收到的美。一旦你播下了它，你就不用担心了。你知道你种在好土里。你知道你会收到你的番茄。控制你的思想就是控制你的生活，控制你的成功。

00:46:29.280 --> 00:46:33.130
<v Jimmy Roy>I'm wanted on the phone. I'm wanted on the phone. Excuse me.
<v Jimmy Roy>电话找我。电话找我。请原谅。

00:46:33.130 --> 00:46:36.820
<v Dan Collison>His regular customers reach him on the Plaza's pay phone to set up appointments.
<v Dan Collison>他的常客会在广场餐厅的公用电话上联系他来预约。

00:46:36.820 --> 00:46:43.620
<v Jimmy Roy>Hello? Yeah, That's be fine. What time? Between 10:00 and 11:00. That'll be fine. Right.
<v Jimmy Roy>喂？是的，那很好。什么时间？10点到11点之间。那很好。好的。

00:46:43.620 --> 00:46:48.750
<v Jimmy Roy>I'm glad you called. I'm glad you're all right. Yeah, we were worried sick about you.
<v Jimmy Roy>我很高兴你打电话。我很高兴你没事。是的，我们为你担心死了。

00:46:48.750 --> 00:47:10.850
<v Dan Collison>Jimmy got his start in sales at the age of 22 in the used car business. Just home from World War II, he was determined to stay out of the steel mills, where he worked as a teenager and where most of his friends were headed. He quit his first sales job after just a week because his dealership was ripping off customers. He's tried to be more honest. He's been a salesman for 54 years.
<v Dan Collison>吉米22岁时在二手车生意中开始了他的销售生涯。刚从二战回家，他决心远离他十几岁时工作且他大多数朋友都去往的钢铁厂。他只做了一周的第一次销售工作就辞职了，因为他的车行在欺骗顾客。他试着表现得更诚实。他已经做了54年的推销员。

00:47:10.850 --> 00:47:16.330
<v Jimmy Roy>Hi, sweetheart. How you doing? How you doing? How you doing?
<v Jimmy Roy>嗨，亲爱的。你好吗？你好吗？你好吗？

00:47:16.330 --> 00:47:19.710
<v Dan Collison>On the way out of the Plaza, Jimmy stops to admire a baby.
<v Dan Collison>在离开广场餐厅的路上，吉米停下来欣赏一个婴儿。

00:47:19.710 --> 00:47:23.400
<v Jimmy Roy>Besides being beautiful, how do you feel? You look great.
<v Jimmy Roy>除了漂亮，你感觉如何？你看上去棒极了。

00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:24.100
<v Mother>Say, "Thank you."
<v Mother>说，“谢谢”。

00:47:24.100 --> 00:47:29.030
<v Jimmy Roy>You look great. You look good. You look like a good girl.
<v Jimmy Roy>你看上去棒极了。你看上去不错。你看上去是个好女孩。

00:47:29.030 --> 00:47:34.360
<v Dan Collison>It's an old salesman trick. Win over the parents by flattering their kids.
<v Dan Collison>这是一个老推销员的把戏。通过奉承他们的孩子来赢得父母的心。

00:47:34.360 --> 00:47:37.070
<v Jimmy Roy>May I give her a lucky dollar?
<v Jimmy Roy>我能给她一个幸运币吗？

00:47:37.070 --> 00:47:38.780
<v Mother>Put it in her piggy bank when we get home.
<v Mother>我们回家后把它放进她的存钱罐里。

00:47:38.780 --> 00:47:39.770
<v Jimmy Roy>Put it in your piggy bank?
<v Jimmy Roy>放进你的小猪存钱罐？

00:47:39.770 --> 00:47:41.410
<v Mother>Or your horsey bank?
<v Mother>还是你的小马存钱罐？

00:47:41.410 --> 00:47:47.000
<v Jimmy Roy>Does she chew bubble gum? I got bubble gum for her.
<v Jimmy Roy>她吃泡泡糖吗？我这儿有泡泡糖给她。

00:47:47.000 --> 00:47:55.720
<v Jimmy Roy>That makes me feel so bad and good. It's unexplainable. I make a baby smile. It makes me feel so great. Honest to god.
<v Jimmy Roy>这让我感觉既糟又好。无法解释。我让一个婴儿笑了。这让我感觉太棒了。向上帝发誓。

00:47:55.720 --> 00:48:10.670
<v Dan Collison>Jimmy drives a gray 1990 Lincoln town car, loaded up with boxes full of bubble gum and fruit exactly for moments like this. He calls them his "giveaways." His dashboard is plastered with scraps of paper printed with motivational aphorisms.
<v Dan Collison>吉米开着一辆灰色的1990年林肯轿车，里面装满了专门为这种时刻准备的泡泡糖和水果盒。他称它们为他的“赠品”。他的仪表盘上贴满了印着励志名言的纸条。

00:48:10.670 --> 00:48:41.880
<v Jimmy Roy>You are very fortunate if you have learned that the most certain way to get is to first give. Choosing to develop an intelligent level of yourself and all of mankind will motivate you to accomplishments above and beyond your expectations. Maintain a positive mental attitude to achieve mental and physical health and to live a longer life. Every day, in every way, I'm getting better and better. And see, all we gotta do is accent the positive.
<v Jimmy Roy>如果你了解到获得的最可靠方法是先给予，那么你非常幸运。选择发展一个理性的自我和全人类水平，将激励你取得超出你预期的成就。保持积极的心态以获得心理和生理健康并活得更长久。每天，在各个方面，我都在变得越来越好。你看，我们所要做的就是强调积极的一面。

00:48:41.880 --> 00:49:05.070
<v Dan Collison>For years, Jimmy Roy has given motivational seminars, where he preaches his philosophy of life. Recently, about 70 people packed the back room at the Plaza Restaurant to hear him give the latest version, entitled "The Secret of Being Rich and Happy." He also gave the seminar to salesmen at Parkway Ford, an auto dealership just up the road from the Plaza. Today, he's back to hand out some cufflinks.
<v Dan Collison>多年来，吉米·罗举办过励志研讨会，向人们宣扬他的生活哲学。最近，大约70人挤满了广场餐厅的后厅，听他发表题为“富有且快乐的秘诀”的最新演讲。他还向广场餐厅路上的Parkway福特汽车经销商的推销员举办了这次研讨会。今天，他回来派发一些袖扣。

00:49:05.070 --> 00:49:06.790
<v Jimmy Roy>That's it. Merry Christmas.
<v Jimmy Roy>就是这样。圣诞快乐。

00:49:06.790 --> 00:49:08.780
<v Car Salesman 1>Thank you, sir. I appreciate it.
<v Car Salesman 1>谢谢，先生。我很感激。

00:49:08.780 --> 00:49:09.210
<v Jimmy Roy>You're welcome.
<v Jimmy Roy>不客气。

00:49:09.210 --> 00:49:12.400
<v Dan Collison>And to see how his motivational talk went over.
<v Dan Collison>看看他的励志演讲效果如何。

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:22.550
<v Car Salesman 2>It was a very, very inspirational speech, very, very motivating. And the guys been rocking ever since. Sales went up.
<v Car Salesman 2>这是一个非常、非常鼓舞人心的演讲，非常、非常激励人。伙计们从那以后一直很带劲。销量上升了。

00:49:22.550 --> 00:49:24.490
<v Jimmy Roy>I'm glad to hear that.
<v Jimmy Roy>我很高兴听到这个。

00:49:24.490 --> 00:49:40.090
<v Dan Collison>It's a little hard to tell whether these salesmen are sincere or whether they're just humoring Jimmy. But they seem to truly like him. And why wouldn't they? The proof is in the numbers. Sales at Parkway Ford did, in fact, increase by 10% after Jimmy's seminar.
<v Dan Collison>很难说这些推销员是真诚的，还是只是在迁就吉米。但他们似乎真的很喜欢他。为什么不呢？证据就在数字里。吉米的研讨会后，Parkway福特的销售额确实增长了10%。

00:49:40.090 --> 00:49:41.500
<v Car Salesman 3>What are some of the things that he told us?
<v Car Salesman 3>他告诉你们的一些事情是什么？

00:49:41.500 --> 00:49:41.790
<v Car Salesman 4>Peace.
<v Car Salesman 4>和平。

00:49:41.790 --> 00:49:45.630
<v Car Salesman 3>I am peace. I am harmony. I am goodwill. I am--
<v Car Salesman 3>我是和平。我是和谐。我是善意。我是——

00:49:45.630 --> 00:49:46.460
<v Car Salesman 4>Poise.
<v Car Salesman 4>镇定。

00:49:46.460 --> 00:49:47.850
<v Car Salesman 3>I am law. I am order.
<v Car Salesman 3>我是法则。我是秩序。

00:49:47.850 --> 00:49:48.640
<v Car Salesman 5>I am spiritual.
<v Car Salesman 5>我是精神性的。

00:49:48.640 --> 00:49:54.220
<v Car Salesman 3>I am spiritual. I'm vitality. I'm understanding. I am successful.
<v Car Salesman 3>我是精神性的。我是活力。我是理解。我是成功的。

00:49:54.220 --> 00:49:54.640
<v Jimmy Roy>And is it working for you?
<v Jimmy Roy>这对你有用吗？

00:49:54.640 --> 00:49:56.950
<v Car Salesman 3>It's working great. He comes by, and man, we pumped up.
<v Car Salesman 3>效果很好。他过来，伙计，我们充满了动力。

00:49:56.950 --> 00:49:59.540
<v Jimmy Roy>You're all pumped up when you leave the house.
<v Jimmy Roy>你们离家时都充满了动力。

00:49:59.540 --> 00:50:00.290
<v Car Salesman 3>Without a doubt.
<v Car Salesman 3>毫无疑问。

00:50:00.290 --> 00:50:14.180
<v Jimmy Roy>See, you feed the mind. The mind is the dynamo that supplies the power. Your thinking is a directing force of that power. And when you have all these things being directed, it's impossible to fail. You cannot fail.
<v Jimmy Roy>你看，你滋养了心灵。心灵是提供能量的发电机。你的思想是那种能量的指挥力量。当你拥有所有这些东西被指挥时，是不可能失败的。你不可能失败。

00:50:14.180 --> 00:50:15.120
<v Car Salesman 4>You cannot fail.
<v Car Salesman 4>你不可能失败。

00:50:15.120 --> 00:50:15.930
<v Jimmy Roy>That's right.
<v Jimmy Roy>说得对。

00:50:15.930 --> 00:50:22.680
<v Dan Collison>Naturally, as he leaves Parkway Ford, there's business to do. One of the car salesmen wants to look at an engagement ring.
<v Dan Collison>自然，当他离开Parkway福特时，有生意要做。其中一位汽车推销员想看看订婚戒指。

00:50:22.680 --> 00:50:25.780
<v Jimmy Roy>That's a marquis in the center.
<v Jimmy Roy>中间是一个马眼钻。

00:50:25.780 --> 00:50:26.690
<v Man Interested In Ring>It's a wide band.
<v Man Interested In Ring>这是一个宽圈。

00:50:26.690 --> 00:50:28.650
<v Jimmy Roy>With the wide band. We have that.
<v Jimmy Roy>带着宽圈。我们有那个。

00:50:28.650 --> 00:50:39.560
<v Dan Collison>Jimmy does deals like this because after 54 years as a salesman, it's who he is. He has to sell. When he tried to quit a few years back, he says, he nearly lost his mind.
<v Dan Collison>吉米做这样的交易，因为在做了54年推销员之后，这就是他。他必须销售。他说，当他几年前试图辞职时，他几乎疯了。

00:50:39.560 --> 00:51:09.420
<v Jimmy Roy>Well, that seems to be a sure sale there because he inquired about it before. And he seems to be very sincere. I can generally tell when it's a sell. I just look at them, and I study-- being that there's only one mind in the universe, his mind and my mind are identical. And if I concentrate hard enough, I can walk right into his mind and feel what he's thinking. It's a great advantage, not to be abused, but to be used.
<v Jimmy Roy>嗯，那看起来是一笔确定的交易，因为他之前打听过。而且他看起来非常真诚。我通常能看出什么时候是一笔生意。我只是看着他们，我研究——既然宇宙中只有一个心灵，他的心灵和我的心灵是完全一样的。如果我集中注意力足够久，我就能直接走进他的心灵，感受他在想什么。这是一个巨大的优势，不是为了滥用，而是为了使用。

00:51:09.420 --> 00:51:11.950
<v Dan Collison>So you're saying, you can actually read his mind.
<v Dan Collison>所以你是说，你真的能读懂他的心思。

00:51:11.950 --> 00:51:26.880
<v Jimmy Roy>Many times. If you concentrate hard enough, you can reach right in there and feel whether he's telling you the truth or he's lying. I believe that all of us have that, but very few use it.
<v Jimmy Roy>很多次。如果你集中注意力足够久，你就能触及那里，感受他是在告诉你真相还是在撒谎。我相信我们所有人都有这个能力，但很少有人使用它。

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:44.520
<v Dan Collison>Jimmy says, he'd like to take his seminar-- "The Secret of Being Rich and Happy"-- on the road. He's writing a book by the same name, though he has no publisher. Of course, Jimmy's philosophy hasn't actually made him rich. And as I hung around him, I kept trying to figure out a polite way to ask him about this.
<v Dan Collison>吉米说，他想带着他的研讨会——“富有且快乐的秘诀”——去巡回。他正在写一本同样名字的书，虽然他没有出版商。当然，吉米的哲学并没有让他真正变得富有。当我在他身边待着时，我一直在想办法找个礼貌的方式问他这个问题。

00:51:44.520 --> 00:51:59.900
<v Dan Collison>Jimmy, there's a question I've been wanting to ask you for a while that's been bothering me a little bit. The name of your seminar and the name of the book you're working on is "The Secret of Being Rich and Happy." But, with all due respect, you're not rich.
<v Dan Collison>吉米，有一个问题我想问你有一段时间了，它让我有点困扰。你的研讨会和你在写的那本书的名字是“富有且快乐的秘诀”。但是，恕我直言，你并不富有。

00:51:59.900 --> 00:52:12.540
<v Jimmy Roy>Well, I think I'm one of the richest men in the world. I'm so abundantly blessed in more respects than money. I have money. I have some money. And I have health and happiness with me. I have things that money can't buy.
<v Jimmy Roy>嗯，我认为我是世界上最富有的人之一。我在金钱以外的许多方面都得到了极其丰富的祝福。我有钱。我有一些钱。我有健康和幸福。我有金钱买不到的东西。

00:52:12.540 --> 00:52:16.310
<v Dan Collison>Why don't you call it "The Secret of Being Happy?"
<v Dan Collison>你为什么不叫它“快乐的秘诀”？

00:52:16.310 --> 00:52:27.950
<v Jimmy Roy>Well, because you're rich when you are happy. We could use that title. But that seemed to come from within me, "The Secret of Becoming Rich and Happy." Well, I'll tell you the truth.
<v Jimmy Roy>嗯，因为当你快乐时你就是富有的。我们可以用那个标题。但这似乎来自我的内心，“富有且快乐的秘诀”。嗯，我告诉你真相。

00:52:27.950 --> 00:52:45.520
<v Jimmy Roy>Another reason why I used the word "rich," I'm going to be very honest, most people desire to be rich. So I figured it would sell more books, to be very honest with you, by them seeing how the secret of becoming rich and happy. I believe it'll sell more books.
<v Jimmy Roy>我使用“富有”这个词的另一个原因，我要非常诚实，大多数人都渴望富有。所以我认为如果你看到“富有且快乐的秘诀”，那会卖出更多的书，跟你说实话。我相信它会卖出更多的书。

00:52:45.520 --> 00:52:52.700
<v Dan Collison>But somehow, I can't believe that you wouldn't want to be more wealthy, that you wouldn't want to be rich.
<v Dan Collison>但无论如何，我无法相信你不想变得更富有，不想变得富有。

00:52:52.700 --> 00:53:10.280
<v Jimmy Roy>Oh, excuse me. I never said I didn't want personal wealth. I think you misunderstood me. I like to have enough, like I have now, to live comfortable, and to eat good, and to have a nice place to stay. And I like money in my pocket.
<v Jimmy Roy>噢，原谅我。我从来没说过我不想要个人财富。我想你误解我了。我喜欢拥有足够的，就像我现在拥有的，过得舒适，吃得好，有地方住。我喜欢口袋里有钱。

00:53:10.280 --> 00:53:22.450
<v Jimmy Roy>I like to give. And I have that. And I don't think there's a richer man in the world than I am as far as the state of mind is concerned. I believe I have the best state of mind in the universe, in my honest to god opinion.
<v Jimmy Roy>我喜欢给予。我拥有这些。我不认为世界上还有比我更富有的人，就心态而言。我相信我有宇宙中最好的心态，以我向上帝发誓的观点。

00:53:22.450 --> 00:53:44.890
<v Dan Collison>By and large, Jimmy does seem genuinely happy. Ask anyone in Braddock. Filmmaker Tony Buba, who's known him just about all his life, says he's never seen Jimmy down. But consider this. The philosophy that's made Jimmy happy may be one of the things that's kept him from being rich. Tony says the reason Jimmy isn't wealthy is because he's too honest.
<v Dan Collison>总的来说，吉米似乎确实很开心。问问布拉多克的任何人。几乎一生都认识他的电影制片人托尼·布巴说，他从没见过吉米低落。但请考虑一下。让吉米快乐的哲学可能是让他无法变得富有的原因之一。托尼说吉米不富有的原因是他太诚实了。

00:53:44.890 --> 00:53:59.760
<v Tony Buba>What I think is he doesn't have that thievery in him. Because I think if he had that thievery, if he had that edge of not really caring about other people, then he probably could have been a millionaire.
<v Tony Buba>我认为他身上没有那种小偷小摸的劲头。因为我想如果他有那种小偷小摸的劲头，如果他有那种不在乎其他人的优势，那么他可能早就成了百万富翁了。

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:19.420
<v Jimmy Roy>I could have probably been a multimillionaire if I wanted to go the wrong way. Because I had the opportunity to cheat people in the automobile business and different businesses I had. But I never would be comfortable. I think I would've been what they call a poor rich man.
<v Jimmy Roy>如果我想走歪门邪道，我可能早就成了千万富翁了。因为我在汽车行业和其他经营的生意中有机会欺骗别人。但我永远不会感到舒服。我想我当时会成为他们所说的“贫穷的富人”。

00:54:19.420 --> 00:54:37.430
<v Tony Buba>People might think, "Oh, Jimmy, Jimmy." Nobody'll walk away from the man. He has a lot of friends. And everybody likes him. Because he's never cheated anybody.
<v Tony Buba>人们可能会想，“哦，吉米，吉米。”没人会远离这个人。他有很多朋友。每个人都喜欢他。因为他从来没欺骗过任何人。

00:54:37.430 --> 00:54:56.970
<v Jimmy Roy>"The Very Thought of You." [SINGING] The very thought of and I forget to do the little ordinary things that everyone ought to do.
<v Jimmy Roy>“想到你”。[唱歌] 想到……我忘了做每个人都应该做的那些小事。

00:54:56.970 --> 00:55:32.080
<v Dan Collison>Our final stop is Moray's Lounge, an upscale piano bar near downtown Pittsburgh. Jimmy comes here to have a few drinks, to listen to Shirley, who's been playing the lounge for almost 20 years, and, every once in a while, to sing a song.
<v Dan Collison>我们的最后一站是莫雷酒廊，匹兹堡市中心附近的一家高档钢琴酒吧。吉米来这里喝几杯酒，听听已经在酒廊演奏了近20年的雪莉的音乐，偶尔唱上一首歌。

00:55:32.080 --> 00:56:22.030
<v Dan Collison>It's tempting to see salesmen like Jimmy Roy as kind of tragic Willy Loman characters. But here at Moray's Lounge, people recognize Jimmy. They call him "Diamond" Jim. He chats with Shirley. He chats with the bartender. His philosophy seems to be working. He looks as happy as a man can be.
<v Dan Collison>把吉米·罗这样的推销员看作威利·洛曼式的悲剧人物很有诱惑力。但在这里的莫雷酒廊，人们认得吉米。他们叫他“钻石”吉米。他和雪莉聊天。他和酒保聊天。他的哲学似乎行得通。他看起来是一个人所能达到的最快乐的样子。

00:56:22.030 --> 00:57:03.270
<v Ira Glass>That story by Dan Collison.
<v Ira Glass>丹·科里森的故事。

00:57:03.270 --> 00:57:17.260
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program produced today by Julie Snyder and myself, with Alex Blumberg, Susan Burton, and Nancy Updike. Contributing editors Paul Tough, Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, Alix Speigel, and Consigliere Sarah Vowell. Production help Jorge Just, Todd Bachmann, and Sylvia Lemus. Marketing by [UNINTELLIGIBLE].
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们今天的节目由朱莉·斯奈德和我制作，亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格、苏珊·伯顿和南希·厄普代克参与。特约编辑保罗·塔夫、杰克·希特、玛吉·罗克林、阿利克斯·斯皮格尔和顾问莎拉·沃威尔。制作协助豪尔赫·贾斯特、托德·巴克曼和西尔维娅·莱姆斯。营销由[听不清]负责。

00:57:17.260 --> 00:57:25.980
